{{smaller block|He [Edward I.], with another engine named the ''warwolf'', pierced with one stone, and cut as even as a thread, two vaunt-mures. {{right|''Camden'', Remains, Artillery, p. 206.}}
<poem>The ''war-wolfs'' there
Hurl’d their huge stones.</poem>
{{right|''Southey'', Joan of Arc, viii.}}}}

'''war-worn''' (wâr′wôrn), ''a.'' Worn with military service: especially applied to a veteran soldier, or one grown old in arms.
{{smaller block|The stout old general whose battles and campaigns are over, who has come home to rest his ''war-worn'' limbs, . . . what must be his feelings? {{right|''Thackeray'', Philip, xvi.}}}}

'''wary'''<sup>1</sup> (wā′ri), ''a.'' [An extended form of ''ware''<sup>1</sup> (< ''ware''<sup>1</sup> + ''-y''<sup>1</sup>), perhaps orig. due to misreading the adv. ''warely'' as a trisyllable.] '''1.''' Cautious of danger; carefully watching and guarding against deception, artifices, and dangers; watchful; on the alert against surprise or danger; ever on one’s guard.
{{smaller block|<poem>Be ''wary'' then; best safety lies in fear.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Hamlet, i. 3. 43.}}
<poem>Are there none here?
Let me look round; we cannot be too ''wary''.</poem>
{{right|''Fletcher'', Rule a Wife, v. 5.}}
All things work for good, and tend to make you more ''wary''. {{right|''Bunyan'', Pilgrim’s Progress, ii.}}}}
'''2.''' Guarded; careful as to doing or not doing something; chary.
{{smaller block|Yet this I can say, I was very ''wary'' of giving them occasion, by any unseemly action, to make them averse to going on pilgrimage. {{right|''Bunyan'', Pilgrim’s Progress, i.}}}}
'''3.''' Characterized by caution; guarded.
{{smaller block|<poem>And in
''Wary'' hypocrisy lets slip her hand
Much farther than she seemed to understand.</poem>}}

